          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly present Goneril's love for Lear as a true emotional state. The rest of her turn intensifies that avowal through comparisons rather than changing its basic truth-claiming force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"The occurrence answers Lear's request to report which daughter loves him most; it does not itself create, renew, or invoke a definite commitment to care for, obey, or support him. Its filial comparisons rhetorically measure affection, but do not position Goneril as undertaking an obligation. Lear's subsequent grant is uptake of her avowal as a successful answer, not acceptance of a promise from her.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.—Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Of all these bounds, even from this line to this,\\nWith shadowy forests and with champains rich’d,\\nWith plenteous rivers and wide-skirted meads,\\nWe make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression is part of an extended, organized, comparative speech delivered in response to a formal solicitation. Its hyperbole and emotional vocabulary do not make the focal words a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love, the occurrence functions as competitive ceremonial praise and a bid to satisfy Lear's love test. Lear has explicitly linked the daughters' answers to the size of his bounty, so Goneril's declaration also has a strategic flattering function not fully captured by T, P, or E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"later_1\",\"quotation\":\"Think’st thou that duty shall have dread to speak,\\nWhen power to flattery bows?\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public royal ceremony, Lear announces that he is dividing his kingdom and asks his daughters to compete in declaring who loves him most, explicitly connecting their answers with his bounty. Goneril speaks first and gives an elaborate declaration of incomparable filial love; Lear treats it as a successful answer and immediately grants her a rich territorial share.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"absent\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"No distinct undertaking by Goneril is established for the focal occurrence. It avows affection and strategically answers Lear's test without specifying or affirming an obligation of future care, obedience, or support. Lear accepts the answer as meritorious and rewards it with land, but this is uptake of the avowal and praise rather than acceptance of a promise. Later supplied discourse strongly challenges the avowal's sincerity, but does not retroactively turn it into or erase a commitment speech act.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied text is recognised as William Shakespeare's King Lear, specifically Goneril's response in the opening love test.\",\"limitations\":\"Actual affection is classified as absent because the supplied material characterizes the performance as flattery and later represents Goneril acting with marked hostility toward Lear. This supports an insincere-avowal reading, though a character's complete inner emotional state cannot be proven directly. The classification is limited to the supplied passages and does not claim that any omitted context is absent from the work.\"}"
        }
